From 6f52a68d14b4375afe55314ac0a66c490aa6f183 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 22:01:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 138/138] gxcore: a compact decoded-vertex layout for draws
 that need no more (60 bytes, from 132)

Every draw was decoded in one 132-byte layout with room for color1, uv2-4,
the tex-matrix indices, binormal and tangent, which few formats carry; the
GX worker wrote it, copied it to the frame's staging, and the GPU copied
it again. Now a draw's key (vertex_layout_full: color1, tex2-4, TEXMTXIDX
or N/B/T in its format) decides between the full layout, the attributes it
shares with the compact one at the same offsets, and a compact one of
pos, posmtx, color0, uv0-1 and normal. A compact pipeline reads the other
inputs from a one-element stream of their defaults (color1 white, the rest
zero), stepped per instance, as the decoder writes them where a full
draw's format lacks them; the ubershader's key marks the full layout with
has_color1 (its WGSL does not depend on it). The key already carried every
bit the choice needs, so stored pipeline configs stay valid.

Vertex bytes uploaded a frame: Forest Haven 13.1 -> 6.0 MB, Hyrule
Castle's room 11.9 -> 5.4, Dragon Roost 8.9 -> 4.0, the sea by the Fortress
8.3 -> 3.8 (DOL_AURORA_GPU_PROF=1). gxcore_tests pass.
